Terms of Use

ERCOT publishes public, secure, and certified market information on its websites at regular intervals. These reports
can be accessed by Market Participants using an Application Programmatic Interface (API), External Web Services
(EWS), ERCOT.com and the Market Information System (MIS). Protocols require that ERCOT ensure all Market Participants
have access to the ERCOT MIS on a nondiscriminatory basis (Section 12).

ERCOT policy requires that query or scan rates not exceed three times the frequency an extract or report is
generated. The ERCOT Market Information List (EMIL) shows how often each extract or report is generated and posted by
ERCOT. Users can find this information under the “Generation Frequency” area of the EMIL.

  EMIL ID | Report Type ID | Product Name | Generating Frequency
  NP6-905-CD | 12301 | Settlement Point Prices at Resource Nodes, Hubs and Load Zones | Chron - 15 Minutes

Example: Query rate for NP6-905-CD should not exceed once every 5 minutes or a maximum of 3 times in 15 minutes.

Violations to This Policy

This policy applies to both:
- Automated screen scraping programs that access the ERCOT URL(s) listed in the table
- Programs that use an API to query and download report data via the API or EWS
The objectives of this policy are to:
- Prevent overloading of ERCOT computing resources that may occur due to excessive queries
- Ensure optimal transactional request/response performance to all Market Participants
Note: Query submission at a faster rate will not provide additional benefits to the Market Participant.

In case of violations to this policy, ERCOT reserves the right to block an offending IP address, or a group of IP
addresses, at the Organizationally Unique Identifier (OUI) level. Further, ERCOT reserves the right to block IP
addresses at the domain level, with the effect that offending systems will not be able to access any ERCOT web pages
or services. The entity will be unblocked once the adjusted queries are in line with this policy.

For questions or clarifications related to this policy, Market Participants may:
- Call their Account Manager directly
- Call Client Services at (512) 248-3900
- Send an email to ClientServices@ercot.com
For questions or clarifications related to technical issues, Market Participants may:
- Call the ERCOT HelpDesk at (512) 248-6800 or 1-866-870-8124
- Send an email to HelpDesk@ercot.com

2. EIA-860: public domain

The battery archetypes (the design table in each manifest's "letters" and the fleet fit in "eia_fit" and stats.md) are
sized from the U.S. Energy Information Administration's Form EIA-860 annual electric generator data, 2025
(eia8602025.zip: Schedule 3_4, Energy Storage, and Schedule 2, Plant), https://www.eia.gov/electricity/data/eia860/,
a work of the U.S. Government. From https://www.eia.gov/about/copyrights_reuse.php, accessed 2026-10-24:

"U.S. government publications are in the public domain and are not subject to copyright protection. You may use
and/or distribute any of our data, files, databases, reports, graphs, charts, and other information products that are
on our website or that you receive through our email distribution service. However, if you use or reproduce any of
our information products, you should use an acknowledgment, which includes the publication date, such as: "Source:
U.S. Energy Information Administration (Oct 2008).""

Source: U.S. Energy Information Administration, Form EIA-860, 2025 data. The batteries in the packs are archetypes
sized from the ERCOT fleet, not any real site. The EIA does not endorse this work.

3. Iowa Environmental Mesonet: attribution

The viewer and the replay Space show each day's weather at the point's representative airport from ASOS observations
archived by the Iowa Environmental Mesonet (IEM), Iowa State University, https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu/. The
observations are U.S. Government (NWS/FAA) data in the public domain; the IEM asks for attribution, given here. They
are fetched into cache/weather and are not in data/ or the Hugging Face dataset.

4. ERCOT data obtained through ERCOT's Public API (v3, v4 and v5 previews)

The v3 regulation drift (data/hybrid/reg_deploy.jsonl.gz) is derived from NP3-965-ER (60-Day SCED Disclosure Reports):
each operating day is reduced to the storage fleet's regulation deployment as a share of its awards, by hour; no
resource is named and the disclosure files are not redistributed. The v4 forecast store carries ERCOT's posted forecasts
as issued: NP3-565-CD (Seven-Day Load Forecast by Model and Weather Zone), NP4-732-CD and NP4-742-CD (wind power
production, system-wide and regional) and NP4-745-CD (solar power production by geographical region). The v5 market days
are derived from NP3-966-ER (60-Day DAM Disclosure Reports) and NP4-523-CD (DAM System Lambda). They were obtained
through ERCOT's Public API (https://apiexplorer.ercot.com/) and are used under ERCOT's terms for that API and the
Website User Agreement quoted above. The v5 days are aggregated: offer curves are cut to at most 64 blocks an hour and
every resource and QSE is replaced by an anonymised id; the disclosure files themselves are not redistributed. Source:
ERCOT. ERCOT does not endorse this work and is not affiliated with it.

5. NOAA HRRR and EIA-930: public domain

The v4 desk forecasts are built from the High-Resolution Rapid Refresh (HRRR) model runs of NOAA's National Centers for
Environmental Prediction, read as issued from the NOAA Open Data Dissemination archive on AWS
(https://registry.opendata.aws/noaa-hrrr-pds/), and from the U.S. Energy Information Administration's Form EIA-930
hourly balancing-authority data for ERCOT (https://www.eia.gov/electricity/gridmonitor/), including its day-ahead
demand forecast. Both are works of the U.S. Government in the public domain. Source: NOAA/NCEP; U.S. Energy
Information Administration. Neither endorses this work.

6. Bulletins

ERCOT market notices (v2 onward) and National Weather Service watches and warnings (from the IEM VTEC archive) appear
in the tasks and replays as paraphrases of their type, level and reason, never as their original wording.
